## Builder capabilities and directives

`BuilderDefinition` is the ergonomic public layer over Moth's canonical
capability surface. It declares source-visible policy. Compiler/build session
state is created later and remains internal.

### One project configuration surface

A builder may declare one canonical project-configuration directive:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    BuilderDefinition::new("com.example.game", BuilderApiVersion::V1)
        .config("game_builder", |config| {
            config.string("asset_root").default("assets");
            config.bool("debug_overlay").default(false);
        })
]

The active driver has already selected the builder. The directive configures
that builder rather than selecting or loading one.

One builder has one project-config schema. A builder cannot register several
unrelated config-only namespaces with precedence rules between them.

The compiler owns directive parsing, argument routing, compile-time folding and
source diagnostics. The SDK helpers build the compiler's schema types rather
than introducing another config parser.

### Primitive build globals

A builder may supply explicitly registered primitive build globals through the
same compiler-owned input contracts. They represent stable semantic
configuration.

They cannot expose the selected backend, operating system, CPU architecture or
another physical target fact to Moth source.

### Register root purposes

Builders declare application entry purposes instead of scanning compiled source
for conventions:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    BuilderDefinition::new("com.example.game", BuilderApiVersion::V1)
        .root_purpose(
            RootPurpose::new("game")
                .directive("game")
                .field("name", BuilderValueType::String),
        )
]

The compiler owns the directive grammar, placement rules, argument typing and
folding. Stage 0 retains validated root-purpose metadata and link planning
creates explicit assemblies from it.

A builder never searches HIR, reserved constants or rendered strings to decide
whether a module is an entry.

A normal root with runtime/output activity but no applicable purpose remains a
diagnostic. A module may create several assemblies only when the registered
purpose contract explicitly permits it.

### Template directives

Template directives use the compiler's existing template-head machinery.
Builders register constrained descriptors for argument shape, body mode,
compatibility and formatter behaviour.

They do not receive arbitrary AST/HIR mutation callbacks. Frontend-owned names
cannot be overridden.

A formatter may transform template content through its documented string
contract. It cannot change declaration identity, visibility, borrow semantics
or executable control flow.

### General builder directives

Builder-owned general directives follow the same rule: the builder declares a
name, legal context, argument signature, attachment form and retained metadata
shape. The compiler performs syntax, placement, typing and folding once.

The builder receives validated metadata. It never receives parser tokens or a
mutable semantic tree to reinterpret.

### Source file kinds

A builder can opt into compiler-known source adapters such as Moth templates and
plain Markdown:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    BuilderDefinition::new("com.example.site", BuilderApiVersion::V1)
        .moth_templates()
        .markdown()
]

This is selection among Moth-supported adapters. It is not a plugin mechanism
for teaching the compiler an arbitrary new language grammar.

Foreign files whose meaning comes from another implementation use an external
import provider instead.

### External import providers

An advanced builder may register a provider for an explicit-extension foreign
file kind. The public provider API exposes stable logical paths, package
registration services, runtime-asset registration and structured diagnostics.

It does not expose the compiler's string/path interners or mutable
`ExternalPackageRegistry` directly.

A provider returns one typed package/runtime description. It cannot reopen Moth
source discovery, parse declarations or mutate an already compiled module.

### Source packages

`SourcePackage` registers a Builder/MothSource package. The package source
remains ordinary Moth and receives the selected boundary `NumericProfile`.

A package that needs project-specific compile-time values uses an explicit
builder synthetic interface with tracked provenance. It does not inherit the
application's private `@project` interface.

### Host packages

`BuilderDefinition::host_package` consumes the semantic projection of a
`moth_host::HostPackage<Env>`. The Rust environment type and runtime adapter are
erased from compiler-facing project state, while the package's stable semantic
identities and target implementations are retained.

The same host-package declaration is installed by the deployed runtime.

### Target implementations

One host binding has one Moth semantic contract and may provide several
physical implementations. Target selection may change implementation but never
the Moth signature.

Each implementation declares a compiler-owned implementation category and its
required capabilities. Examples include a Rust/Wasm host import and a
registered JavaScript module export.

The set of implementation categories is closed. Builders cannot install an
arbitrary HIR/backend callback or custom lowerer. A new physical integration
requires a Moth-owned implementation category.

Package names do not imply target affinity. Target validation consumes the
registered implementation and capability metadata.

If a reachable binding has no compatible implementation, or its required host
capability is unavailable, compilation reports a target diagnostic before
lowering.

### Definition validation

`finish` performs cross-registration checks after local Rust macro checks have
already run. Definition diagnostics include:

- invalid or conflicting builder identity/API version
- package, namespace, function, constant and type collisions
- attempts to override compiler-owned names
- more than one builder config surface
- invalid config defaults or root-purpose schemas
- conflicting target implementations
- source-kind/provider conflicts
- incompatible host-package semantic definitions

These are structured SDK diagnostics rather than opaque strings. They identify
the relevant registration names and the corrective action where practical.
